  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "One animal killed over sixty people in three years. It hunted a single corner of France and would not stop.",
        "visual_query": "rural french mountain hills",
        "visual_fallback": "rolling hills"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The attacks began in Gevaudan in summer 1764 and ran for three years, across ninety kilometres of mountains. Reports counted over two hundred attacks.",
        "visual_query": "margeride plateau france landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "upland plateau"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Louis XV sent soldiers, then professional wolf hunters with eight bloodhounds, then his own hunter Francois Antoine. How does one animal beat all of them?",
        "visual_query": "french forest hunting hounds",
        "visual_fallback": "forest hounds"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Antoine shot a huge grey wolf in September 1765, sixty kilos and nearly two metres long, and was paid nine thousand livres. The attacks started again.",
        "visual_query": "european grey wolf forest",
        "visual_fallback": "grey wolf"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A local hunter, Jean Chastel, killed the last one in June 1767. Historians now think it was never one monster, just wolves, and a province that had stopped sleeping.",
        "visual_query": "misty french highland pasture",
        "visual_fallback": "misty pasture"
      }
